\item both halves of $S$ are nonempty.
\end{enumerate}

The second column of \eqref{diagtriangle} shows that the nonzero cohomology $G$-modules of~$L_d$ are $\mathbb{Z}(d-1)$ in degrees $-1$ and $0$, so one has a distinguished triangle 
\begin{equation}
\label{sesL}
\mathbb{Z}(d-1)[1]\to L_d\to\mathbb{Z}(d-1)\to.
\end{equation}
The class of this extension is given by a morphism $\mathbb{Z}(d-1)\to\mathbb{Z}(d-1)[2]$, which corresponds to a cohomology class $\alpha\in\mathrm{H}^2(G,\mathbb{Z})=\mathbb{Z}/2$. The claim is then that this class is trivial. Suppose on the contrary that $\alpha$ is the nontrivial element of $\mathrm{H}^2(G,\mathbb{Z})$. As the connecting homomorphisms \[\mathrm{H}^j(G,\mathbb{Z}(d-1))\to\mathrm{H}^{j+2}(G,\mathbb{Z}(d-1))\] of the long exact sequence induced by \eqref{sesL} are given by the cup product with $\alpha$, they are isomorphisms for all $j\geq 1$. It follows that~$\mathrm{H}^j(G,L_d)$ vanishes for $j\geq 2$. 

However, the second row of \eqref{diagtriangle} shows that $\mathrm{H}^j(G,K_d)$ vanishes for $j\geq 1$, and the third column of \eqref{diagtriangle} therefore implies that $\mathrm{H}^j(G,L_d)$ is nonzero for $j\geq 1$. This is the required contradiction.

