\label{th3bis}
Let $X$ be a smooth variety over $\mathbb{R}$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)] 
\item
\label{th3bisi}
 Suppose that any class~${\alpha\in \mathrm{H}^4_G(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))}$ of coniveau $\geq 2$ that is of the form $2\beta$ for some ${\beta\in \mathrm{H}^4_G(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))}$ is in fact of the form~$2\beta'$ for some~${\beta'\in\ker(\operatorname{ev}^4_X)}$. Then $\operatorname{\overline{ev}}^3_X$ and~$\operatorname{\overline{ev}}^3_{\mathrm{nr},X}$ have the same image.
 \item 
\label{th3bisii}
Suppose that any class $\alpha\in \mathrm{H}^4(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})$ of coniveau $\geq 2$ that lifts to a class in~${\mathrm{H}^4_G(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))}$ in fact lifts to a class in $\ker(\operatorname{ev}^4_X)$. Then $\operatorname{ev}^3_X$ and~$\operatorname{ev}^3_{\mathrm{nr},X}$ have the same image.
\end{enumerate}

We treat both assertions simultaneously, using the short exact sequence
\begin{equation}
\label{sosNP}
0\to\mathbb{Z}(4)\xrightarrow{a} N\to P\to 0,
\end{equation}
where $(N,P)=(\mathbb{Z}(4),\mathbb{Z}/2)$ in case \ref{th3bisi}, and $(N,P)=(\mathbb{Z}[G],\mathbb{Z}(3))$ in case \ref{th3bisii} (in the latter case, the exact sequence \eqref{sosNP} is a special case of \eqref{rcses}). The image of $\operatorname{ev}^3_{X,P}$ is obviously contained in that of $\operatorname{ev}^3_{\mathrm{nr},X,P}$, and we prove the converse.

Fix $\gamma\in\mathrm{H}^3_{\mathrm{nr}}(X,P)$. The unramified cohomology group $\mathrm{H}^3_{\mathrm{nr}}(X,P)$ can be described as the subgroup of $\operatorname{colim}_V \mathrm{H}^{3}_G(V(\mathbb{C}),P)$ (where $V$ runs over all dense open subsets of $X$) consisting of those classes whose residues along all codimension $1$ integral subvarieties of $X$ vanish (see \cite[(5.1) and (5.2)]{benoistIntegralHodgeConjecture2020}). It follows that there exists a closed subset $Z\subset X$ of codimension $\geq 2$, with complement $U:=X\setminus Z$, such that~$\gamma$ is represented by a class $\delta\in\mathrm{H}^3_G(U(\mathbb{C}),P)$.

Denote by $\partial$ the boundary maps of \eqref{sosNP}. Choose a closed subset $Z'\subset Z$ of codimension $\geq 3$ in $X$ such that $Y:=Z\setminus Z'$ is smooth of pure codimension $2$ in~$U':=X\setminus Z'$. In view of equivariant purity (see \cite[(1.21)]{benoistIntegralHodgeConjecture2020}), the long exact sequence of cohomology with support in $Y$ reads
\begin{equation}
\label{lescohosupport}
\mathrm{H}^4_G(U'(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))\to \mathrm{H}^4_G(U(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))\to \mathrm{H}^1_G(Y(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}).
\end{equation}
As $\partial(\delta)$ is killed by $2$ (this is obvious in case \ref{th3bisi} and follows from~\eqref{rcles} in case~\ref{th3bisii}), its image in the torsion free group $\mathrm{H}^1_G(Y(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})$ vanishes, so \eqref{lescohosupport} shows that $\partial(\delta)$ lifts to $\mathrm{H}^4_G(U'(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))$. Iterating the argument (by choosing a closed subset~$Z''\subset Z'$ of codimension $\geq 4$ in $X$ such that $Z'\setminus Z''$ is smooth of pure codimension~$3$ in~$X\setminus Z''$, etc.) implies that $\partial(\delta)\in\mathrm{H}^4_G(U(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))$ lifts to a class~$\beta\in\mathrm{H}^4_G(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(4))$.

As $(a_*\beta)|_U=a_*\partial(\delta)=0$, the class $a_*\beta$ has coniveau $\geq 2$. The hypothesis (applied with $\alpha=a_*\beta$) therefore gives $\beta'\in\ker(\operatorname{ev}_X^4)$ with $a_*\beta'=a_*\beta$. By exactness of the long exact sequence attached to \eqref{sosNP}, we deduce the existence of $\varepsilon \in\mathrm{H}^3_G(X(\mathbb{C}),P)$ such that $\partial(\varepsilon)=\beta-\beta'$. Restricting to $U$ yields $\partial(\varepsilon)|_U=\partial(\delta)-\beta'|_U$. Since the boundary map $\partial$ induces an isomorphism $\mathrm{H}^3(G,P)\ext@arrow 0359\arrowfill@\relbar\relbar{\setbox\z@\hbox{$\rightarrow$}\dimen0\ht\z@\multiply\dimen0 6\divide\dimen0 10\ht\z@\dimen0\box\z@}{}{\,\sim\,}\mathrm{H}^4(G,\mathbb{Z}(4))$, we deduce that $\operatorname{ev}^3_{X,P}(\varepsilon)|_{U(\mathbb{R})}=\operatorname{ev}^3_{U,P}(\delta)=\operatorname{ev}^3_{\mathrm{nr},X,P}(\gamma)|_{U(\mathbb{R})}$. As $U(\mathbb{R})$ is dense in~$X(\mathbb{R})$ by smoothness of $X$, we get~$\operatorname{ev}^3_{X,P}(\varepsilon)=\operatorname{ev}^3_{\mathrm{nr},X,P}(\gamma)$, which completes the proof.

\label{corth3bis}
Let $X$ be a smooth proper variety over $\mathbb{R}$. Suppose that any Hodge class in $\mathrm{H}^4(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})$

lifts to $\ker(\operatorname{ev}_X^4)$. Then~$\operatorname{\overline{ev}}_X^3$ and $\operatorname{\overline{ev}}_{\mathrm{nr},X}^3$ have the same image.

\label{remH4nr}
Theorem \ref{th3bis} and its proof exploit the deep relation between degree~$3$ unramified cohomology classes and the coniveau $\geq 2$ property for degree $4$ cohomology classes (which is, for instance, used fruitfully in \cite{colliot-theleneCohomologieNonRamifiee2012}).
To prove an analogue of Theorem \ref{th3bis} for degree $4$ unramified cohomology classes, one might similarly try to exploit their relation to Griffiths groups of codimension $3$ cycles (see~\cite{voisinDegree4Unramified2012}). Unfortunately, we could not find a criterion that we are able to apply to any real algebraic variety of interest.

When $d=2$, combine Theorem \ref{thm:topological_argument_integral_case} and Corollary \ref{corpetitdegre}.

Suppose now that $d=3$. Then $\operatorname{ev}^3_X$ is not surjective by Theorem~\ref{thm:topological_argument_integral_case}. To conclude the proof of the theorem, we check the hypothesis of Theorem \ref{th3bis}\,\ref{th3bisii}. In doing so, it is convenient to identify the canonically isomorphic $G$\nobreakdash-modules $\mathbb{Z}$, $\mathbb{Z}(2)$ and $\mathbb{Z}(4)$. 

Fix ${\alpha\in \mathrm{H}^4_G(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))}$ whose image~$\beta\in \mathrm{H}^4(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})$ has coniveau $\geq 2$, and hence is algebraic. As $\mathrm{H}^*(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})$ is torsion free, the K\"unneth decomposition reads
\begin{equation}
\label{Kunneth}
\mathrm{H}^4(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})=\bigoplus_{i=2}^4\mathrm{H}^{i}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})\otimes_\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{H}^{4-i}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}).
\end{equation}
Let $(\beta_2,\beta_3,\beta_4)$ be the image of $\beta$ in \eqref{Kunneth}. As $E$ is very general, no nonzero class in $\mathrm{H}^{3}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})\otimes_\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{H}^{1}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})\simeq \mathrm{H}^1(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}/2)$ is algebraic (see \cite[Theorem~0.1]{benoistOttemFailureIHC2020}). To be precise, this reference only states that one of these classes is not algebraic; this implies our stronger assertion because the monodromy of the universal family of complex elliptic curves acts transitively on the nonzero classes in~$\mathrm{H}^1(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}/2)$. Since the two other factors of \eqref{Kunneth} are entirely algebraic, one has~$\beta_3=0$.

Since $\beta=\beta_2+\beta_4$ lifts to $\mathrm{H}^4_G(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))$, it belongs to the $G$-invariant part of 
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{H}^{2}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))\otimes_\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{H}^{2}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))\oplus\mathrm{H}^{4}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))\otimes_\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{H}^{0}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}).
\end{equation*}
As the groups $\mathrm{H}^{0}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})$ and $\mathrm{H}^{2}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))$, as well as $\mathrm{H}^{4}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))$, carry a trivial action of $G$, the class $\beta=\beta_2+\beta_4$ in fact belongs to 
\begin{equation}
\label{Kunneth3}
\mathrm{H}^{2}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))^G\otimes_\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{H}^{2}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))\oplus\mathrm{H}^{4}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))\otimes_\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{H}^{0}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}).
\end{equation}
The four groups appearing in \eqref{Kunneth3} are spanned by classes of algebraic cycles defined over $\mathbb{R}$. Indeed, the groups $\mathrm{H}^{2}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))=\mathbb{Z}$ and ${\mathrm{H}^{4}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))=\mathbb{Z}}$ are generated by the class of a point (and $E$ and $S$ have real points), and $\mathrm{H}^{0}(E(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})$ is generated by the class of $E$. As for $\mathrm{H}^{2}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))^G$, consider the composition
$$\operatorname{Pic}(S)\to \mathrm{H}^{2}_G(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))\to \mathrm{H}^{2}(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(1))^G$$
of Krasnov's equivariant cycle class map (for which see \cite{krasnovEquivariantGrothendieckCohomology1994} or \cite[\S 1.6.1]{benoistIntegralHodgeConjecture2020}) and of the natural morphism and note that the former is surjective by \cite[Proposition 2.8]{benoistIntegralHodgeConjecture2020} as~$\mathrm{H}^2(S,\mathcal{O}_S)=0$, and the latter is surjective by the Hochschild--Serre spectral sequence (see \cite[(1.4)]{benoistIntegralHodgeConjecture2020}) as $\mathrm{H}^1(S(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z})=0$ and $S(\mathbb{R})\neq\varnothing$.

This proves that $\beta\in\mathrm{H}^{4}(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))$ lifts to a class $\gamma\in\mathrm{H}^{4}_G(X(\mathbb{C}),\mathbb{Z}(2))$ that is algebraic (in the sense that it is in the image of the equivariant cycle class map). Being algebraic, the class $\gamma$ vanishes when restricted to any real point of~$X$.

\label{propcubic}
Let $X$ be a smooth cubic hypersurface of dimension $d$ over~$\mathbb{R}$ such that~$X(\mathbb{R})$ is not connected. If $d\in\{1,2,3\}$, or if $d=4$ and $X$ is very general,
then~$\operatorname{\overline{ev}}^{d-1}_{\mathrm{nr},X}$ is not surjective. If~$d\geq 5$, then $\operatorname{ev}^{d-1}_{\mathrm{nr},X}$ is surjective.

By pullback along the reduction mod~$2$ morphism $\iota_*\mathbb{Z}(\mathcal{L})\to\iota_*\mathbb{Z}/2$, the section $\overline{\sigma}_\mathrm{nr}$ of $\overline{\operatorname{ev}}_\mathrm{nr}^d$ constructed in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:functorial_Krasnov} induces a section
\begin{equation}
\label{sectiontau}
\iota_*\mathbb{Z}(\mathcal{L})\to\mathscr{H}_X^d(\mathbb{Z}/2)\times_{(\iota_*\mathbb{Z}/2)}\iota_*\mathbb{Z}(\mathcal{L})\simeq\mathbf{I}^d(\mathcal{L})
\end{equation}
of $\operatorname{sign}_d$ (where the isomorphism in \eqref{sectiontau} stems from the fiber product square \eqref{eq:milnor_conjecture2}). By functoriality of cohomology, the morphism $\operatorname{sign}_{d,X}^c$ obtained from $\operatorname{sign}_d$ by taking cohomology also has a section.

Since $n\leq d$, the sheaf $\mathbf{I}^d(\mathcal{L})$ is a subsheaf of $\mathbf{I}^n(\mathcal{L})$. By definition of the signature homomorphisms (see \cite[(2-4)]{lerbetImageHigherSignature2026}), the inclusion $\mathbf{I}^d(\mathcal{L})\subseteq\mathbf{I}^n(\mathcal{L})$ induces a commutative square
\begin{equation}
\label{signdn}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep=small]
	{\mathrm{H}^c(X,\mathbf{I}^d(\mathcal{L}))} & {\mathrm{H}^c(X,\mathbf{I}^n(\mathcal{L}))} \\
	{\mathrm{H}^c(X(\mathbb{R}),\mathbb{Z}(\mathcal{L}))} & {\mathrm{H}^c(X(\mathbb{R}),\mathbb{Z}(\mathcal{L})).}
	\arrow[from=1-1, to=1-2]
	\arrow["{\operatorname{sign}_{d,X}^c}"', from=1-1, to=2-1]
	\arrow["{\,\operatorname{sign}_{n,X}^c}", from=1-2, to=2-2]
	\arrow["{\cdot 2^{d-n}}"', from=2-1, to=2-2]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Since $\operatorname{sign}_{d,X}^c$ is surjective by Corollary \ref{cor:integral_cycle_class_map_split_surjective}, the square \eqref{signdn} implies that the image of $\operatorname{sign}_{n,X}^c$ contains the multiples of $2^{d-n}$. This proves the theorem.

\label{propPfistercubic}
Let $X\subset \mathbb{P}^{d+1}_{\mathbb{R}}$ be a smooth cubic hypersurface of dimension~$d\geq 5$. 
Then $\operatorname{sign}^0_{d-1,X}:\mathrm{H}^0(X,\mathbf{I}^{d-1})\to\mathrm{H}^0(X(\mathbb{R}),\mathbb{Z})$ is surjective. As a consequence, the cokernel of the signature morphism $\mathrm{W}_{\mathrm{nr}}(X)\to \mathrm{H}^0(X(\mathbb{R}),\mathbb{Z})$ is killed by~$2^{d-1}$.

Let $p:\mathbb{P}_X^1\to X$ and $q:\mathbb{P}_X^1\to \mathbb{P}^1_\mathbb{R}$ be the projections.
Let $i:X\to\mathbb{P}_X^1$ be the closed immersion induced by the point at infinity of $\mathbb{P}^1_{\mathbb{R}}$, and let $j:\mathbb{A}_X^1\hookrightarrow\mathbb{P}_X^1$ be the complementary open immersion. We also denote by $r:\mathbb{A}_X^1\to X$ the projection. 

Consider the localization long exact sequence in $\mathbf{I}$-cohomology
{\small
\begin{equation*}
\cdots \to \mathrm{H}^c(X,\mathbf{I}^n(\omega_{X/\mathbb{P}_X^1}\otimes\mathcal{L}))
\xrightarrow{i_*}
\mathrm{H}^{c+1}(\mathbb{P}_X^1,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(p^*\mathcal{L}))
\xrightarrow{j^*}
\mathrm{H}^{c+1}(\mathbb{A}_X^1,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(r^*\mathcal{L}))
\to\cdots
\end{equation*}
}
stemming from \cite[Theorem 2.41]{hornbostelRealCycleClass2021}. One computes that
$$\omega_{X/\mathbb{P}_X^1}=\omega_{X/\mathbb{R}}\otimes i^*\omega_{\mathbb{P}_X^1/\mathbb{R}}^{-1}=\omega_{X/\mathbb{R}}\otimes
i^*(p^*\omega_{X/\mathbb{R}}\otimes q^*\omega_{\mathbb{P}_\mathbb{R}^1/\mathbb{R}})^{-1}\simeq\mathcal{O}_X,$$
where the last isomorphism holds as $p\circ i=\operatorname{Id}$ and $q\circ i$ is constant. In addition, for all $m\geq 0$, since $r^*=j^*\circ p^*$ and $r^*:\mathrm{H}^m(X,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(\mathcal{L}))\to\mathrm{H}^m(\mathbb{A}_X^1,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(r^*\mathcal{L}))$ is an isomorphism by \cite[Théorème 11.2.9]{faselGroupesChowWitt2008}, the morphism $\sigma:=p^*\circ(r^*)^{-1}$ is a section of~$j^*:\mathrm{H}^m(\mathbb{P}_X^1,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(p^*\mathcal{L}))\to\mathrm{H}^m(\mathbb{A}_X^1,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(r^*\mathcal{L}))$ which is therefore surjective. It follows that the above localization exact sequence induces a short exact sequence
\begin{equation*}
0\to\mathrm{H}^c(X,\mathbf{I}^n(\mathcal{L}))
\xrightarrow{i_*}
\mathrm{H}^{c+1}(\mathbb{P}_X^1,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(p^*\mathcal{L}))
\xrightarrow{j^*}
\mathrm{H}^{c+1}(\mathbb{A}_X^1,\mathbf{I}^{n+1}(r^*\mathcal{L}))
\to0
\end{equation*}
which is split by $\sigma$. An entirely similar argument based on the localization exact sequence, on the Thom isomorphism, and on homotopy invariance for cohomology with locally constant coefficients yields a short exact sequence
\begin{equation*}
